{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/berea/jobs/4564378/adjunct-faculty": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty Location Berea Job Type Adjunct Division Dean of Faculty Opening Date 07/01/2024 FLSA Exempt Description Benefits Please note that references provided at the time of application for staff positions will not be contacted until the applicant has been offered the position. Description: Berea College welcomes applications for a part-time adjunct instructor position. The successful candidate will have a minimum of a master’s degree in their field, a clear commitment to undergraduate liberal arts education and the ability to actively engage undergraduate students in the classroom. Previous college-level teaching experience is strongly preferred. We are seeking an individual with experience working within a community of diverse students and colleagues, who is familiar with evidence-based, student-centered teaching and committed to creating an inclusive classroom environment. The successful candidate will, through their interview and prior record of teaching, research, and/or service, demonstrate that they can contribute to the diversity and excellence of our community. To apply, send a cover letter stating the academic department(s) and course(s) you are prepared to teach in, a current CV with your mailing address, unofficial transcripts, and the name and email contact for two references. Your application will be kept in a pool in an ongoing process whereby applications are held from year to year. When there is a need that matches your application, you will be contacted. THERE IS NOT A SPECIFIC POSITION ASSOCIATED WITH THIS POSTING. THIS POSTING WILL BE USED TO CREATE A DATABASE OF POTENTIAL PART-TIME FACULTY. Founded in 1855, Berea College is nationally recognized as the first coeducational and interracial college in the South. Berea has a longstanding commitment to interracial education and is one of the most racially diverse private liberal arts colleges in the United States. With an emphasis on service to Appalachia and beyond, Berea enrolls more than 1,500 students from 44 states and U.S. territories and more than 60 countries. Berea College admits students who are unable to afford tuition and provides every enrolled student with a fully funded degree that covers 100% of tuition, housing, food, supplies, and fees without loans, for all four years. Berea’s students excel in the College’s supportive yet demanding academic environment, and most are the first in their families to attend college. As one of only 10 federally recognized Work Colleges, all Berea students work 10-12 hours weekly in various positions across campus, building professional skills in a real-world learning environment. Washington Monthly ranked Berea College the #1 Best College for your Tuition and Tax Dollars, 2025. Berea also ranks #1 in educational access by The New York Times , 2023 and #1 for lowest educational debt by U.S. News and World Report , 2025 and 2026. Berea College is also the only institution in Kentucky to receive the 5-Star Rating from Money Magazine, 2026. Located where the Bluegrass Region meets the Cumberland Mountains, the town of Berea (pop. 17,000) lies 40 miles south of Lexington and is approximately two hours from Cincinnati, Louisville, and Knoxville. More information about Berea College is available at www.berea.edu . Berea College, in light of its mission in the tradition of impartial love and social equality, welcomes all people of the earth to learn and work here. This position is not eligible for benefits, however, Berea College still offers unique position opportunities you can read more about below. Our Unique Culture: Berea College strives to be a place where people with various Christian interpretations, different religious traditions, and no religious traditions work together in support of Berea's Great Commitments. Over the past century, various leaders of the College have applied the College’s inclusive scriptural foundation and spirit to their expanding world and welcomed those whose beliefs were consistent with the Christian gospel of impartial love. Therefore, Berea College today affirms its inclusive Christian tradition even as it respects the traditions of Jews, Muslims, Buddhists, and Hindus, as well as other faiths, and those holding no religious beliefs. We strive not to ignore our differences, but rather seek to understand each other honestly and respectfully, and together create a climate where anyone can openly discuss what they believe without fear of sanction. To that end, all persons who are willing to share in the spirit and the work of the Great Commitments as shaped by its preamble are welcome to study, to teach, and to work at Berea Labor Supervision: In support of Berea College’s great commitments, staff and faculty serve as labor supervisors. Our fourth great commitment, The Dignity of Labor, promotes learning and serving in community through the student Labor Program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Labor Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving student work, developing students' workplace acumen, and providing tangible work experience. To learn more about the labor program office please see the following link Labor Program Office. Employer Berea College Address 101 Chestnut St. Berea, Kentucky, 40404 Website http://www.berea.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/berea/jobs/5483289/assistant-professor-of-child-and-family-studies": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Child and Family Studies",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Child and Family Studies Location Berea Job Type Tenure-Track Division Provost Department Child and Family Studies Opening Date 09/15/2026 FLSA Exempt Labor Supervisor? Yes Description Benefits Please note that references provided at the time of application for staff positions will not be contacted until the applicant has been offered the position. Description: Berea College announces a tenure-track faculty appointment in the Department of Child and Family Studies (CFS) beginning August 2027. The CFS department offers three concentration areas: Child Development, Family Studies, and Nutrition and Food Studies. This position requires a doctoral degree in human development/family studies, family and consumer sciences, social work, or a related field. Applicants with ABD status are encouraged to apply if they can complete all doctoral degree requirements by the start of the tenure-track appointment. Evidence of teaching experience at the undergraduate level is essential. The successful candidate will teach six courses per academic year.They will be responsible for creating and teaching a proposed new core course on family systems, ecological, and human development theories that will serve as an introduction to the major each term. They will also share teaching CFS 207 (Family Relations) each year and elective courses such as CFS 245 (Community Resources for Families), CFS 350 (Family Law and Policy), and CFS 362 (Contemporary Family Issues). It is highly desirable that the candidate teach cross-content in Child Development as needed and contribute to the Learning and Inquiry program by offering courses in family systems and relationships applicable to students in all disciplines. Prior experience or interest in teaching service-learning, internships, or other high-impact learning practices are highly recommended. Successful candidates are expected to meet the College’s four standards for tenure and promotion: (1) teaching excellence, (2) research and scholarship, (3) student advising and mentorship, and (4) service to the department, division, and Berea College, and as appropriate, the profession, the Berea community, the Appalachian Region, and beyond. The College’s high- support educational model expects candidates to advise a diverse student body, including both traditional and non-traditional domestic students and international students. Following long-standing tradition and practice at Berea College, this position includes responsibility for supervising, mentoring, and developing students in the College's integrated Work College Program. This supports student learning, career readiness, and the program's learning goals and outcomes. The Work College Program provides a developmental framework that guides student learning while creating a structured mentorship opportunity, with role-specific training that equips faculty and staff mentors to support student development. Faculty and staff mentors play an essential part in helping students connect their work-learning-service experiences with broader academic, professional, and personal development. For faculty, this aligns with Berea's tenure and promotion criteria. The CFS Department maintains a well-established presence and reputation on campus. CFS ranks consistently among the top five academic programs and fosters an inclusive culture of mentoring diverse students. The major serves students in other programs including Nursing, Psychology, Sociology, and Women’s, Gender, and Sexuality Studies. The appointment offers opportunities for cross-disciplinary engagement with undergraduates and colleagues across diverse disciplines. The successful candidate must demonstrate a willingness to appreciate, understand, contribute to, interact with, and teach within a diverse intellectual and cultural residential community grounded in the College’s mission and the Great Commitments. Candidates should submit a cover letter, a current curriculum vitae, graduate transcripts, a teaching philosophy, a research/scholarship statement that includes a post-doctoral productivity timeline through tenure and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin October 19, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Founded in 1855, Berea College is nationally recognized as the first coeducational and interracial college in the South. Berea has a longstanding commitment to interracial education and is one of the most racially diverse private liberal arts colleges in the United States. With an emphasis on service to Appalachia and beyond, Berea enrolls more than 1,500 students from 44 states and U.S. territories and more than 60 countries. Berea College admits students who are unable to afford tuition and provides every enrolled student with a fully funded degree that covers 100% of tuition, housing, food, supplies, and fees without loans, for all four years. Berea’s students excel in the College’s supportive yet demanding academic environment, and most are the first in their families to attend college. As one of only 10 federally recognized Work Colleges, all Berea students work 10-12 hours weekly in various positions across campus, building professional skills in a real-world learning environment. Washington Monthly ranked Berea College the #1 Best College for your Tuition and Tax Dollars, 2025. Berea also ranks #1 in educational access by The New York Times , 2023 and #1 for lowest educational debt by U.S. News and World Report , 2025 and 2026. Berea College is also the only institution in Kentucky to receive the 5-Star Rating from Money Magazine, 2026. Located where the Bluegrass Region meets the Cumberland Mountains, the town of Berea (pop. 17,000) lies 40 miles south of Lexington and is approximately two hours from Cincinnati, Louisville, and Knoxville. More information about Berea College is available at www.berea.edu . Berea College, in light of its mission in the tradition of impartial love and social equality, welcomes all people of the earth to learn and work here. Our Benefits: Berea College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees and their families including health care, dental, vision, retirement, health savings accounts, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, short-term disability, long-term disability and an Employee Assistance Program. The benefit package also includes access to the Child Development Laboratory for children of College Staff, ages 6 weeks to 6 years, Seabury Center Membership, Staff Development Funds and Tuition Reimbursement. Eligible staff employees also receive paid holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of twenty-four hours per week over the course of a year, receive benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page at https://www.berea.edu/human-resources/employee-benefits . Our Unique Culture: Berea College strives to be a place where people with various Christian interpretations, different religious traditions, and no religious traditions work together in support of Berea's Great Commitments. Over the past century, various leaders of the College have applied the College’s inclusive scriptural foundation and spirit to their expanding world and welcomed those whose beliefs were consistent with the Christian gospel of impartial love. Therefore, Berea College today affirms its inclusive Christian tradition even as it respects the traditions of Jews, Muslims, Buddhists, and Hindus, as well as other faiths, and those holding no religious beliefs. We strive not to ignore our differences, but rather seek to understand each other honestly and respectfully, and together create a climate where anyone can openly discuss what they believe without fear of sanction. To that end, all persons who are willing to share in the spirit and the work of the Great Commitments as shaped by its preamble are welcome to study, to teach, and to work at Berea Labor Supervision: In support of Berea College’s great commitments, staff and faculty serve as labor supervisors. Our fourth great commitment, The Dignity of Labor, promotes learning and serving in community through the student Labor Program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Labor Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving student work, developing students' workplace acumen, and providing tangible work experience. To learn more about the labor program office please see the following link Labor Program Office. Employer Berea College Address 101 Chestnut St. Berea, Kentucky, 40404 Website http://www.berea.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/berea/jobs/5474364/assistant-professor-of-computer-science-tenure-track-or-3-yr-fixed-term-positio": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Computer Science - Tenure Track OR 3-YR Fixed Term Position",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Computer Science - Tenure Track OR 3-YR Fixed Term Position Location Berea Job Type Full-Time Division Provost Department Computer Science Opening Date 09/08/2026 FLSA Exempt Labor Supervisor? Yes Description Benefits Please note that references provided at the time of application for staff positions will not be contacted until the applicant has been offered the position. Description: Two New Faculty Positions in Computer Science at Berea College The Department of Computer Science at Berea College invites applications for two new full-time faculty positions beginning August 2027: a tenure-track Assistant Professor and a 3-Year Post-Graduate, Post-Doctoral, or Visiting Assistant Professor. These positions reflect sustained growth in Computer and Information Science, now one of the College's largest majors, and our commitment to an interdisciplinary, liberal arts-grounded computing program. We seek colleagues enthusiastic about teaching, mentoring, and scholarship in a department dedicated to active learning, inclusive pedagogy, and connecting computing to human, ethical, and societal contexts. Applicants with a terminal degree by August 2027 in computer science or a closely related field may apply for a newly created tenure-track Assistant Professor position. Applicants with a master’s degree in computer science or a closely related field are invited to apply for a three-year faculty position with the possibility of continuance, depending on institutional need and performance. Rank for the three-year position may be post-graduate, post-doctoral, or visiting, depending on qualifications and experience. Candidates may apply for either or both positions and are encouraged to list their preference in the cover letter. The CS Department values small class sizes, close student-faculty interaction, and support for students from varied backgrounds, including those historically underrepresented in computing, as they develop their skills, confidence, and sense of purpose. Colleagues will have the flexibility and support needed to teach across the undergraduate curriculum, develop courses in their areas of specialization, make use of highly flexible spaces in the new Computing, Media, and IT building, and pursue research with undergraduate students. All specialty areas in computing are welcome, with particular interest in areas of strong student demand, including artificial intelligence, machine learning, cybersecurity, data science, and computer science education. Applicants should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, institutional transcripts, and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin October 14, 2026, and continue until the positions are filled. The department is a collegial community of faculty and staff who work closely with students as learners, advisees, teaching assistants, lab assistants, and student software developers through Berea’s distinctive Work College program. Following long-standing tradition and practice at Berea College, this position includes responsibility for supervising, mentoring, and developing students in the College's integrated Work College Program. This supports student learning, career readiness, and the program's learning goals and outcomes. The Work College Program provides a developmental framework that guides student learning while creating a structured mentorship opportunity, with role-specific training that equips faculty and staff mentors to support student development. Faculty and staff mentors play an essential part in helping students connect their work-learning-service experiences with broader academic, professional, and personal development. For faculty, this aligns with Berea's tenure and promotion criteria. These Work College relationships have helped create a strong alumni network that mentors current students professionally. Founded in 1855, Berea College is nationally recognized as the first coeducational and interracial college in the South. Berea has a longstanding commitment to interracial education and is one of the most racially diverse private liberal arts colleges in the United States. With an emphasis on service to Appalachia and beyond, Berea enrolls more than 1,500 students from 44 states and U.S. territories and more than 60 countries. Berea College admits students who are unable to afford tuition and provides every enrolled student with a fully funded degree that covers 100% of tuition, housing, food, supplies, and fees without loans, for all four years. Berea’s students excel in the College’s supportive yet demanding academic environment, and most are the first in their families to attend college. As one of only 10 federally recognized Work Colleges, all Berea students work 10-12 hours weekly in various positions across campus, building professional skills in a real-world learning environment. Washington Monthly ranked Berea College the #1 Best College for your Tuition and Tax Dollars, 2025. Berea also ranks #1 in educational access by The New York Times , 2023 and #1 for lowest educational debt by U.S. News and World Report , 2025 and 2026. Berea College is also the only institution in Kentucky to receive the 5-Star Rating from Money Magazine, 2026. Located where the Bluegrass Region meets the Cumberland Mountains, the town of Berea (pop. 17,000) lies 40 miles south of Lexington and is approximately two hours from Cincinnati, Louisville, and Knoxville. More information about Berea College is available at www.berea.edu . Berea College, in light of its mission in the tradition of impartial love and social equality, welcomes all people of the earth to learn and work here. Our Benefits: Berea College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees and their families including health care, dental, vision, retirement, health savings accounts, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, short-term disability, long-term disability and an Employee Assistance Program. The benefit package also includes access to the Child Development Laboratory for children of College Staff, ages 6 weeks to 6 years, Seabury Center Membership, Staff Development Funds and Tuition Reimbursement. Eligible staff employees also receive paid holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of twenty-four hours per week over the course of a year, receive benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page at https://www.berea.edu/human-resources/employee-benefits . Our Unique Culture: Berea College strives to be a place where people with various Christian interpretations, different religious traditions, and no religious traditions work together in support of Berea's Great Commitments. Over the past century, various leaders of the College have applied the College’s inclusive scriptural foundation and spirit to their expanding world and welcomed those whose beliefs were consistent with the Christian gospel of impartial love. Therefore, Berea College today affirms its inclusive Christian tradition even as it respects the traditions of Jews, Muslims, Buddhists, and Hindus, as well as other faiths, and those holding no religious beliefs. We strive not to ignore our differences, but rather seek to understand each other honestly and respectfully, and together create a climate where anyone can openly discuss what they believe without fear of sanction. To that end, all persons who are willing to share in the spirit and the work of the Great Commitments as shaped by its preamble are welcome to study, to teach, and to work at Berea Labor Supervision: In support of Berea College’s great commitments, staff and faculty serve as labor supervisors. Our fourth great commitment, The Dignity of Labor, promotes learning and serving in community through the student Labor Program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Labor Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving student work, developing students' workplace acumen, and providing tangible work experience. To learn more about the labor program office please see the following link Labor Program Office. Employer Berea College Address 101 Chestnut St. Berea, Kentucky, 40404 Website http://www.berea.edu/ Apply"
