    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/berea/jobs/5486455/assistant-professor-of-health-and-human-performance-dance-3yr-appointment-non-t": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Health and Human Performance Dance – 3YR Appointment Non-Tenure/Continuing",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Health and Human Performance Dance – 3YR Appointment Non-Tenure/Continuing Location Berea Job Type Fixed-Term Division Provost Department Health & Human Performance Opening Date 09/17/2026 FLSA Exempt Labor Supervisor? Yes Description Benefits Please note that references provided at the time of application for staff positions will not be contacted until the applicant has been offered the position. Description: Position Overview and Department Description: The Berea College Health and Human Performance Department announces a three-year appointment in Dance beginning August 2027. The Department offers B.A. degrees in Health and Human Performance, HHP P-12 Teaching Certification, Health Studies, and minors in Health Studies and Dance, providing a wide range of exercise science, health, and wellness courses that serve the entire student body. We seek an enthusiastic dance educator with a strong commitment to undergraduate liberal arts education who demonstrates a commitment to carrying out the mission of Berea College. The ideal candidate displays professionalism across the campus community and thrives when working with students who possess significant artistic potential with limited dance experience. Following long-standing tradition and practice at Berea College, this position includes responsibility for supervising, mentoring, and developing students in the College's integrated Work College Program. This supports student learning, career readiness, and the program's learning goals and outcomes. The Work College Program provides a developmental framework that guides student learning while creating a structured mentorship opportunity, with role-specific training that equips faculty and staff mentors to support student development. Faculty and staff mentors play an essential part in helping students connect their work-learning-service experiences with broader academic, professional, and personal development. For faculty, this aligns with Berea's tenure and promotion criteria. Teaching and Responsibilities: Teach core courses within the Dance Minor and General Education perspectives in the Arts. Potential coursework includes World Dance, Dance History, Dance Appreciation, and/or Dance Movement as a Therapeutic Practice. Instruct dance technique courses aligned with the candidate’s area of expertise and departmental need (e.g., jazz, tap/percussive, ballet, improvisation, traditional, ballroom, musical theatre, social, folk, hip hop, street dance, or other popular dance genres). Direct a dance ensemble and mentor student dance groups. Maintain active creative engagement in composition and/or performance through participation in annual departmental dance concerts and/or external opportunities. The ability to teach general education Wellness is beneficial but not required. Qualifications: Required: Terminal degree (MFA or Ph.D.) in Dance. Faculty rank and salary will be commensurate with education, experience, and creative/scholarly record. Application Process and Materials: Candidates should submit a cover letter, a current curriculum vitae, graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review), a teaching philosophy, a research/creative project plan or artistic statement, and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin October 23, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Founded in 1855, Berea College is nationally recognized as the first coeducational and interracial college in the South. Berea has a longstanding commitment to interracial education and is one of the most racially diverse private liberal arts colleges in the United States. With an emphasis on service to Appalachia and beyond, Berea enrolls more than 1,500 students from 44 states and U.S. territories and more than 60 countries. Berea College admits students who are unable to afford tuition and provides every enrolled student with a fully funded degree that covers 100% of tuition, housing, food, supplies, and fees without loans, for all four years. Berea’s students excel in the College’s supportive yet demanding academic environment, and most are the first in their families to attend college. As one of only 10 federally recognized Work Colleges, all Berea students work 10-12 hours weekly in various positions across campus, building professional skills in a real-world learning environment. Washington Monthly ranked Berea College the #1 Best College for your Tuition and Tax Dollars, 2025. Berea also ranks #1 in educational access by The New York Times , 2023 and #1 for lowest educational debt by U.S. News and World Report , 2025 and 2026. Berea College is also the only institution in Kentucky to receive the 5-Star Rating from Money Magazine, 2026. Located where the Bluegrass Region meets the Cumberland Mountains, the town of Berea (pop. 17,000) lies 40 miles south of Lexington and is approximately two hours from Cincinnati, Louisville, and Knoxville. More information about Berea College is available at www.berea.edu . Berea College, in light of its mission in the tradition of impartial love and social equality, welcomes all people of the earth to learn and work here. Our Benefits: Berea College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees and their families including health care, dental, vision, retirement, health savings accounts, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, short-term disability, long-term disability and an Employee Assistance Program. The benefit package also includes access to the Child Development Laboratory for children of College Staff, ages 6 weeks to 6 years, Seabury Center Membership, Staff Development Funds and Tuition Reimbursement. Eligible staff employees also receive paid holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of twenty-four hours per week over the course of a year, receive benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page at https://www.berea.edu/human-resources/employee-benefits . Our Unique Culture: Berea College strives to be a place where people with various Christian interpretations, different religious traditions, and no religious traditions work together in support of Berea's Great Commitments. Over the past century, various leaders of the College have applied the College’s inclusive scriptural foundation and spirit to their expanding world and welcomed those whose beliefs were consistent with the Christian gospel of impartial love. Therefore, Berea College today affirms its inclusive Christian tradition even as it respects the traditions of Jews, Muslims, Buddhists, and Hindus, as well as other faiths, and those holding no religious beliefs. We strive not to ignore our differences, but rather seek to understand each other honestly and respectfully, and together create a climate where anyone can openly discuss what they believe without fear of sanction. To that end, all persons who are willing to share in the spirit and the work of the Great Commitments as shaped by its preamble are welcome to study, to teach, and to work at Berea Labor Supervision: In support of Berea College’s great commitments, staff and faculty serve as labor supervisors. Our fourth great commitment, The Dignity of Labor, promotes learning and serving in community through the student Labor Program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Labor Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving student work, developing students' workplace acumen, and providing tangible work experience. To learn more about the labor program office please see the following link Labor Program Office. Employer Berea College Address 101 Chestnut St. Berea, Kentucky, 40404 Website http://www.berea.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/berea/jobs/5455652/assistant-professor-of-nursing-tenure-track": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Nursing Tenure-Track",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Nursing Tenure-Track Location Berea Job Type Tenure-Track Division Provost Department Nursing Opening Date 08/20/2026 FLSA Exempt Labor Supervisor? Yes Description Benefits Please note that references provided at the time of application for staff positions will not be contacted until the applicant has been offered the position. Description: Berea College’s Nursing Department, the oldest program west of the Alleghenies, announces a tenure-track or continuing full-time position to begin in August 2026. The successful candidate will have a teaching record in Adult Health. A doctorate degree is preferred. A Master of Science in Nursing degree is required. Candidates must also have a current unencumbered Kentucky RN license. Preference will be given to candidates with simulation experience. Candidates must be committed to excellent undergraduate teaching in a pre-licensure baccalaureate nursing program and have an interest in contributing to the College’s general education program when possible. Following long-standing tradition and practice at Berea College, this position includes responsibility for supervising, mentoring, and developing students in the College's integrated Work College Program. This supports student learning, career readiness, and the program's learning goals and outcomes. The Work College Program provides a developmental framework that guides student learning while creating a structured mentorship opportunity, with role-specific training that equips faculty and staff mentors to support student development. Faculty and staff mentors play an essential part in helping students connect their work-learning-service experiences with broader academic, professional, and personal development. For faculty, this aligns with Berea's tenure and promotion criteria. Candidates should submit a cover letter, a current curriculum vitae, all academic transcripts, a teaching philosophy and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin October 20, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Your Key Responsibilities: The successful candidate will have a teaching record in Adult Health. A doctorate degree is preferred. A Master of Science in Nursing degree is required. Candidates must also have a current unencumbered Kentucky RN license. Preference will be given to candidates with simulation experience. Candidates must be committed to excellent undergraduate teaching in a pre-licensure baccalaureate nursing program and have an interest in contributing to the College’s general education program when possible. Founded in 1855, Berea College is nationally recognized as the first coeducational and interracial college in the South. Berea has a longstanding commitment to interracial education and is one of the most racially diverse private liberal arts colleges in the United States. With an emphasis on service to Appalachia and beyond, Berea enrolls more than 1,500 students from 44 states and U.S. territories and more than 60 countries. Berea College admits students who are unable to afford tuition and provides every enrolled student with a fully funded degree that covers 100% of tuition, housing, food, supplies, and fees without loans, for all four years. Berea’s students excel in the College’s supportive yet demanding academic environment, and most are the first in their families to attend college. As one of only 10 federally recognized Work Colleges, all Berea students work 10-12 hours weekly in various positions across campus, building professional skills in a real-world learning environment. Washington Monthly ranked Berea College the #1 Best College for your Tuition and Tax Dollars, 2025. Berea also ranks #1 in educational access by The New York Times , 2023 and #1 for lowest educational debt by U.S. News and World Report , 2025 and 2026. Berea College is also the only institution in Kentucky to receive the 5-Star Rating from Money Magazine, 2026. Located where the Bluegrass Region meets the Cumberland Mountains, the town of Berea (pop. 17,000) lies 40 miles south of Lexington and is approximately two hours from Cincinnati, Louisville, and Knoxville. More information about Berea College is available at www.berea.edu . Berea College, in light of its mission in the tradition of impartial love and social equality, welcomes all people of the earth to learn and work here. Our Benefits: Berea College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees and their families including health care, dental, vision, retirement, health savings accounts, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, short-term disability, long-term disability and an Employee Assistance Program. The benefit package also includes access to the Child Development Laboratory for children of College Staff, ages 6 weeks to 6 years, Seabury Center Membership, Staff Development Funds and Tuition Reimbursement. Eligible staff employees also receive paid holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of twenty-four hours per week over the course of a year, receive benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page at https://www.berea.edu/human-resources/employee-benefits . Our Unique Culture: Berea College strives to be a place where people with various Christian interpretations, different religious traditions, and no religious traditions work together in support of Berea's Great Commitments. Over the past century, various leaders of the College have applied the College’s inclusive scriptural foundation and spirit to their expanding world and welcomed those whose beliefs were consistent with the Christian gospel of impartial love. Therefore, Berea College today affirms its inclusive Christian tradition even as it respects the traditions of Jews, Muslims, Buddhists, and Hindus, as well as other faiths, and those holding no religious beliefs. We strive not to ignore our differences, but rather seek to understand each other honestly and respectfully, and together create a climate where anyone can openly discuss what they believe without fear of sanction. To that end, all persons who are willing to share in the spirit and the work of the Great Commitments as shaped by its preamble are welcome to study, to teach, and to work at Berea Labor Supervision: In support of Berea College’s great commitments, staff and faculty serve as labor supervisors. Our fourth great commitment, The Dignity of Labor, promotes learning and serving in community through the student Labor Program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Labor Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving student work, developing students' workplace acumen, and providing tangible work experience. To learn more about the labor program office please see the following link Labor Program Office. Employer Berea College Address 101 Chestnut St. Berea, Kentucky, 40404 Website http://www.berea.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/berea/jobs/5471453/assistant-professor-of-sociology-tenure-track": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Sociology Tenure-Track",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Sociology Tenure-Track Location Berea Job Type Tenure-Track Division Provost Department Sociology Opening Date 09/03/2026 FLSA Exempt Labor Supervisor? Yes Description Benefits Please note that references provided at the time of application for staff positions will not be contacted until the applicant has been offered the position. Description: The Sociology Department at Berea College invites applicants with expertise in quantitative research methods for a full-time, tenure-track position with appointment beginning in August 2027. Primary teaching responsibilities will consist of Research Methods, Statistics, and other courses in the candidate’s area of expertise. While the primary disciplinary focus of this position is Sociology, candidates holding a terminal degree in Social Work are also encouraged to apply, provided they have the methodological expertise required for the position. All faculty at Berea are also expected to contribute to the General Education Program, advise, and mentor students, engage in ongoing professional and scholarly growth, and provide service to the Department and College. Full-time course load is three courses per semester. Candidates must have completed a PhD or the equivalent in Sociology or Social Work by time of appointment (August 1, 2027). Following long-standing tradition and practice at Berea College, this position includes responsibility for supervising, mentoring, and developing students in the College's integrated Work College Program. This supports student learning, career readiness, and the program's learning goals and outcomes. The Work College Program provides a developmental framework that guides student learning while creating a structured mentorship opportunity, with role-specific training that equips faculty and staff mentors to support student development. Faculty and staff mentors play an essential part in helping students connect their work-learning-service experiences with broader academic, professional, and personal development. For faculty, this aligns with Berea's tenure and promotion criteria. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a current curriculum vitae, graduate transcripts, a teaching philosophy, summary of teaching evaluations, a statement of research interests, and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin October 15th and continue until the position is filled. Required Qualifications: Expertise in quantitative methods Experience with quantitative methods Evidence of successful college-level teaching, especially courses in statistics and research methods Demonstrated commitment to working with a diverse student population Demonstrated ability to mentor students Demonstrated commitment to the liberal arts Willingness to contribute to the General Education program, in particular with the teaching of undergraduate writing seminars. Desired Qualifications: Experience with R and/or other open-access statistical packages. Your Key Responsibilities: Primary teaching responsibilities will consist of Research Methods, Statistics, and other courses in the candidate’s area of expertise. While the primary disciplinary focus of this position is Sociology, candidates holding a terminal degree in Social Work are also encouraged to apply, provided they have the methodological expertise required for the position. All faculty at Berea are also expected to contribute to the General Education Program, advise, and mentor students, engage in ongoing professional and scholarly growth, and provide service to the Department and College. Full-time course load is three courses per semester. Candidates must have completed a PhD or the equivalent in Sociology or Social Work by time of appointment (August 1, 2027). Following long-standing tradition and practice at Berea College, this position includes responsibility for supervising, mentoring, and developing students in the College's integrated Work College Program. This supports student learning, career readiness, and the program's learning goals and outcomes. The Work College Program provides a developmental framework that guides student learning while creating a structured mentorship opportunity, with role-specific training that equips faculty and staff mentors to support student development. Faculty and staff mentors play an essential part in helping students connect their work-learning-service experiences with broader academic, professional, and personal development. For faculty, this aligns with Berea's tenure and promotion criteria. What You'll Bring: Required Qualifications: Expertise in quantitative methods Experience with quantitative methods Evidence of successful college-level teaching, especially courses in statistics and research methods Demonstrated commitment to working with a diverse student population Demonstrated ability to mentor students Demonstrated commitment to the liberal arts Willingness to contribute to the General Education program, in particular with the teaching of undergraduate writing seminars. Desired Qualifications: Experience with R and/or other open-access statistical packages. Founded in 1855, Berea College is nationally recognized as the first coeducational and interracial college in the South. Berea has a longstanding commitment to interracial education and is one of the most racially diverse private liberal arts colleges in the United States. With an emphasis on service to Appalachia and beyond, Berea enrolls more than 1,500 students from 44 states and U.S. territories and more than 60 countries. Berea College admits students who are unable to afford tuition and provides every enrolled student with a fully funded degree that covers 100% of tuition, housing, food, supplies, and fees without loans, for all four years. Berea’s students excel in the College’s supportive yet demanding academic environment, and most are the first in their families to attend college. As one of only 10 federally recognized Work Colleges, all Berea students work 10-12 hours weekly in various positions across campus, building professional skills in a real-world learning environment. Washington Monthly ranked Berea College the #1 Best College for your Tuition and Tax Dollars, 2025. Berea also ranks #1 in educational access by The New York Times , 2023 and #1 for lowest educational debt by U.S. News and World Report , 2025 and 2026. Berea College is also the only institution in Kentucky to receive the 5-Star Rating from Money Magazine, 2026. Located where the Bluegrass Region meets the Cumberland Mountains, the town of Berea (pop. 17,000) lies 40 miles south of Lexington and is approximately two hours from Cincinnati, Louisville, and Knoxville. More information about Berea College is available at www.berea.edu . Berea College, in light of its mission in the tradition of impartial love and social equality, welcomes all people of the earth to learn and work here. Our Benefits: Berea College offers a comprehensive benefits package to eligible employees and their families including health care, dental, vision, retirement, health savings accounts, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, short-term disability, long-term disability and an Employee Assistance Program. The benefit package also includes access to the Child Development Laboratory for children of College Staff, ages 6 weeks to 6 years, Seabury Center Membership, Staff Development Funds and Tuition Reimbursement. Eligible staff employees also receive paid holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of twenty-four hours per week over the course of a year, receive benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page at https://www.berea.edu/human-resources/employee-benefits . Our Unique Culture: Berea College strives to be a place where people with various Christian interpretations, different religious traditions, and no religious traditions work together in support of Berea's Great Commitments. Over the past century, various leaders of the College have applied the College’s inclusive scriptural foundation and spirit to their expanding world and welcomed those whose beliefs were consistent with the Christian gospel of impartial love. Therefore, Berea College today affirms its inclusive Christian tradition even as it respects the traditions of Jews, Muslims, Buddhists, and Hindus, as well as other faiths, and those holding no religious beliefs. We strive not to ignore our differences, but rather seek to understand each other honestly and respectfully, and together create a climate where anyone can openly discuss what they believe without fear of sanction. To that end, all persons who are willing to share in the spirit and the work of the Great Commitments as shaped by its preamble are welcome to study, to teach, and to work at Berea Labor Supervision: In support of Berea College’s great commitments, staff and faculty serve as labor supervisors. Our fourth great commitment, The Dignity of Labor, promotes learning and serving in community through the student Labor Program, honoring the dignity and utility of all work, mental and manual, and taking pride in work well done. Student Labor Supervision includes scheduling, assigning, and approving student work, developing students' workplace acumen, and providing tangible work experience. To learn more about the labor program office please see the following link Labor Program Office. Employer Berea College Address 101 Chestnut St. Berea, Kentucky, 40404 Website http://www.berea.edu/ Apply"
